      "text": "To find the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6, we can begin by finding the greatest 3 digit number and then keeping in mind that 6 is a factor of 30. It follows that the least value divisible by both 3 and 6 is 30. So, we take this and decrease it three times by 30 to get 270 as the result.\n\nTo check further that this value is the greatest three-digit number divisible by 3 and 6, we can take the next smaller value by subtracting 30 repeatedly, 270 - 120, 270 -240, 270 - 360= 0, which is 0. Hence, 270 contains more than one factor of 3 (105 times) and 280 contains only two factors of 3 (9 boxes are for the prime factor 3 which thus confirms the absence of 30 as the desired result). Therefore, the greatest number should be divisible by 30:\n\n$$\\frac{280 + 30}{30} > \\frac{240 + 60}{60} = 28 > 4 > 8 > \\frac{180 - 30}{6}= 25$$\n\n$$> \\frac{555 - 150}{15} > \\frac{475 -127}{15} > \\frac{320-141}{15} > \\frac{378 - 246}{15} > \\frac{324 - 150}{15} > \\frac{315 - 180}{15} = 12 > 9 > 6> 3$$\n\n► Using PEMDAS, we can then go on to solve the fractions, we see that 180, 120, and 60 shall result in 4, 8, and 9. We then reduce parentheses from 240 and 270- 180 = 90. Hence, we must divide by 2, which gives 4-9. The fourth fraction reduces to 8-3 with five fractions left to be solved. The only solution to that fraction is one more solution for 12, making a total of 9 solutions:\n\n18.9 > 7 < 6 > 0\n\nThus, the largest number that satisfies all of the given requirements is 270.\n\nIt is known that the missing digit is $6$.",
